  "query_preview": "..\n\nNotably milder conditions arrive tonight, established within a \nmeaningful window of warm air advection under persistent low level \nsouthwest flow. This pattern emerging within the immediate \ndownstream moderating warm sector ahead of an inbound cold front set \nto arrive Wednesday. This in combination with periodic thicker mid \nlevel cloud lends to low temperatures Wednesday morning arriving a \nsolid +10-15 degrees warmer than that noted this morning. Existing \nsystem relative isentropic ascent lacks in greater moisture quality, \nwhile better forcing remains displaced to the north. There remains a \nsmall subset of the model solution space that offer a lower \nprobability for a few widely scattered showers/sprinkles to develop \novernight, mainly across the Saginaw Bay region and northern thumb.  \n\nModest height falls emanating south of a closed system lifting thru \nHudson Bay will draw the associated lead cold front across southeast \nMichigan Wednesday. Limited moisture quality and lack of larger \nscale dynamics or frontal convergence still suggest a dry frontal \npassage. Daytime heating effectively counters any meaningful initial \ncold air advection during the diurnal heating cycle. This will allow \nhighs to push into the lower to middle 70s. Some modest late day \ngustiness to the post-frontal westerly wind. Secondary stronger cold \nfrontal passage set for Wednesday night as the main vorticity lobe \nglances across the northern great lakes. This may again offer a lower\nprobability for shower production mainly north of I-69 during the \novernight hours. Outgoing forecast holding with dry conditions at \nthis stage. \n\nBroad, high-latitude surface ridge centered near northern Ontario \nwill expand southward into the Great Lakes basin Thursday, becoming \nfully entrenched over Southeast Michigan Thursday night into Friday. \nThe deep layer stability held within this environment affords dry \nconditions to finish the work week. Coolest conditions Thursday as \n850 mb temps dip into the lower single digits within the immediate \npost-frontal environment. Strong radiational cooling prospects \nThursday night likely bring low temperatures back into the 30s for \nsome locations. Strengthening southerly flow within the background \nof an aggressive increase in upper level heights will then lead to a \nmore significant warming trend for this weekend. This will push \ntemperatures well above early October normals by Sunday as readings \nclimb well into the 70s. Strong ridging aloft ensures dry conditions \nthrough at least the first half of the weekend. Attention for the \nback half of the weekend will focus on behavior of a deeper plume of \nmoisture currently projected to accompany a stronger mid level \ncirculation out of the western gulf. An upward revision in forecast \nprecipitation chances plausible Sunday as confidence in system \ntrajectory improves.",
